350 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 240 0 R
/T (Where support did come for VC-backed IPOs was in the initial offering price. Many of the companies were priced within or above their estimated price range. Those ranges were generally set to valuations much lower than those the companies had received in the private market. After all, companies are expected to be priced relative to current market conditions. This suggests that banks did, in fact, price these offerings accordingly. The IPO prices of these listing companies held up much stronger than their private market valuations or the initial pops. )
/E (Where support did come for VC-backed IPOs was in the initial offering price. Many of the companies were priced within or above their estimated price range. Those ranges were generally set to valuations much lower than those the companies had received in the private market. After all, companies are expected to be priced relative to current market conditions. This suggests that banks did, in fact, price these offerings accordingly. The IPO prices of these listing companies held up much stronger than their private market valuations or the initial pops. )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
351 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 240 0 R
/T (Poor postlisting performance tells us several things. One is that first-day pops are more about manufactured hype than true market sentiment. Most of the unicorns had strong debuts, with half pricing their listing above their estimated range. The second is that private market pricing appears to be increasingly misaligned with )
/E (Poor postlisting performance tells us several things. One is that first-day pops are more about manufactured hype than true market sentiment. Most of the unicorns had strong debuts, with half pricing their listing above their estimated range. The second is that private market pricing appears to be increasingly misaligned with )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
352 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 240 0 R
/T (In 2025, just completing an IPO was the goal; hopes for a bellwether year were set aside. Companies that completed public offerings during the year largely did not perform well after their listings. As of December 31, almost 70% of the newly listed companies were trading below their first-day close. Half were trading below their IPO share sale price. )
/E (In 2025, just completing an IPO was the goal; hopes for a bellwether year were set aside. Companies that completed public offerings during the year largely did not perform well after their listings. As of December 31, almost 70% of the newly listed companies were trading below their first-day close. Half were trading below their IPO share sale price. )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
353 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 240 0 R
/T (There were exceptions. CoreWeave ended the year up 79% from its IPO price. However, its trading was volatile. At one point, the stock was up more than 250%, and at another, it was down more than 7%. The overall poor performance of VC-backed companies challenges the narrative that public investors are supporting risky tech companies in the current market. AI-induced growth has provided strong performance for megacap stocks, offering growth-return exposure from proven businesses that are also at the forefront of AI. That has likely induced negative performance for the AI IPO cohort. Why risk investing in a developing company with high losses when strong returns can be generated through an investment in Google or Microsoft? Overall, AI IPOs did not perform spectacularly in 2025. Just two listings ended the year up more than 20% from their IPO price, and of the five highest-performing stocks, only two trended positively over the year. By December 31, only four of the stocks were above their IPO price. )
/E (There were exceptions. CoreWeave ended the year up 79% from its IPO price. However, its trading was volatile. At one point, the stock was up more than 250%, and at another, it was down more than 7%. The overall poor performance of VC-backed companies challenges the narrative that public investors are supporting risky tech companies in the current market. AI-induced growth has provided strong performance for megacap stocks, offering growth-return exposure from proven businesses that are also at the forefront of AI. That has likely induced negative performance for the AI IPO cohort. Why risk investing in a developing company with high losses when strong returns can be generated through an investment in Google or Microsoft? Overall, AI IPOs did not perform spectacularly in 2025. Just two listings ended the year up more than 20% from their IPO price, and of the five highest-performing stocks, only two trended positively over the year. By December 31, only four of the stocks were above their IPO price. )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [6 7]
>>
endobj
